it is great to be joined today by Peter  simi who is a professor of sociology at  Chapman University also co-author with  Robert Petrella of the book American  swastika inside the white power  movements hidden spaces of hate so much  to talk about particularly now but maybe  we'll start sort of with how you first  started researching in in this area from  a pretty early age I read you were sort  of aware of and kind of cataloguing  racist incidents that you would observe  how did you first get into this well  yeah  so starting pretty young around maybe  the age about nine I did do that you  know I wasn't doing research per se cuz  I didn't know what that was I was  noticing things in my environment that  you know appeared to be racist and and  that was something an interest that was  sparked in part by growing up with they  come an older brother / father figure  who's african-american and kind of seen  racist things kind of looking at it from  his perspective and then also my mother  kind of at a young age taught me about  hate groups and kind of the history of  hate groups and we watched a PBS  documentary about the Ku Klux Klan and a  pretty young age that made a big impact  on me and so you know starting starting  early on I was just trying to really  understand kind of why there are these  kind of beliefs and feelings about  different groups based on racial  differences or religious differences or  sexual orientation but really in grad  school about 1996 I started formally  studying hate groups and then in the  winter of 97 I actually started spending  time with active members of hate groups  so let's let's go to that I mean how  does that start because you know our  audience will remember when I  interviewed Darryl Davis who spends time  with KKK members neo-nazis white  supremacist without sort of trying he  has his relationships have led to many  of them abandoning their beliefs talk a  little bit about you not as a black man  like  Harold Davis how you started spending  time with these groups yeah I mean  Darryl's work is amazing  but his approach is a little bit  different because I'm coming out more as  a kind of a researcher trying to  understand these individuals and the  groups that they belong to on their  terms so I'm I'm trying to build  relationships in a way to where they  feel comfortable kind of opening up to  me about how they got involved and what  their you know thoughts are and views  are about the world so I'm using a very  kind of approach where I'm trying to  build rapport where I'm trying to make  them feel like in some ways I might be  sympathetic to them and so there's you  know some ethical issues obviously  involved with that but it I felt it was  necessary for folks to feel comfortable  to feel like they weren't being judged  by me to kind of you know you're not in  agreement with things you don't agree  with you you know you you listen a lot  and and and really you know don't offer  necessarily any critical feedback about  things in order for them to kind of open  up to you and so that that process for  me you know I started by writing a  letter to a group they had a Pio box  this is 1997 so it's the early days of  the internet and this group responded  back I told them basically I wanted to  meet real people involved with these  groups and compare it to media  demonization and I used those words on  purpose because I knew that already that  they felt you know they basically  victimized they feel like the media and  others you know view them and in a  particular way that kind of stigmatizes  them and and marginalizes them and so  they have this real strong sense of  victimization and so I wanted to try and  appeal to that basically in hopes that  that might resonate with them and it  seemed to and they said they'd be  willing to meet with me and so from  there we met in person and then they  after the initial meeting they said I  could come back and stay with them in  their homes and crash on their you know  couch in the front room type of thing  sand when you're with some of these  individuals other than the white  supremacist neo-nazi KKK whatever  beliefs is it otherwise sort of like  just hanging out with your friends or  are there some other sort of key  differences that  pervade their entire day-to-day you know  it's a little bit of both I mean there  are some eerie you're kind of eerie  similarities where you feel like wow  this is a lot like hanging out with  people I've went to school with and and  there's some similarities there on the  other hand you know there's for for the  folks that are deeply involved and  strongly committed to these views and  the larger movement I mean it really is  a lifestyle to where it permeates their  everyday living in the sense of where  they decorate their homes in some cases  with say portraits of Hitler or you know  have Nazi banners that kind of thing  around the home they watch TV in a  particular way so they filter the news  through there kind of white supremacist  lens so even watching a TV show can  become a big you know there where  they're kind of complaining about  multiculturalism they're complaining  about liberalism that kind of thing yeah  you know going to the grocery store can  become an ordeal because they happen to  you know get cut off in the driver  coincidentally happens to be someone of  a different racial or ethnic group and  this turns into a big you know commotion  so in a way that there's certain  similarities in a way that you know  because their lifestyle is such that  where they're so committed to these  beliefs  it really does distinguish them in some  pretty dramatic ways when I read the  book Donnie Brasco which of course is  the retelling by former undercover FBI  agent Joe Pistone of his time undercover  with the New York different families of  the New York Mafia he talks about being  around homophobic comments and  anti-semitic comments and that type of  thing and sort of adopting an attitude  of kind of like a nonchalance and and  kind of like indifference he doesn't  participate but again he's playing a  character as an undercover FBI agent do  you feel as though when you are with  these individuals in a sense you're  playing a character because you are  being upfront about the fact that you're  doing research at the same time you're  not reacting to each comment in the way  you would maybe as an academic what's  your sense of your role in that in that  way  I think there are some interesting  similarities there I mean you do I think  you know we all play roles in different  situations that we developed to kind of  navigate that situation or adapt to the  environment and in this particular  environment I felt like I had to do that  to be able to get along and so yeah I  did laugh at jokes that I thought were  disgusting you know I did not an  approval about ideas that the white race  is on the verge of extinction you know I  don't believe that obviously but these  are things that I had to kind of feign  agreement about I didn't run around I  wasn't doing Nazi salutes or dressing  like you know in their uniforms or  things like that but there were  occasions even where depending on the  gathering you know in some circumstances  people actually just assumed that I was  part of their group I was part of a  different group that was associated with  with the event and so in some cases  people just assumed that I was one of  them and if they would you know if they  if they made that assumption clear to me  then I would correct them but if they  didn't then I would just let them assume  whatever they assumed so you know and in  some of the gatherings where there's a  lot you know fairly large number of  people obviously it's not possible for  everyone to know that you're you're  there doing research so some folks just  assume that you're an attendee like they  are so it did require developing a kind  of certain persona that was not that was  very different than obviously Who I am  really yeah you know in in an authentic  sense so what did your research turn up  about the sort of entryway into these  beliefs I've interviewed Scott Shepherd  former KKK Frankie mink who's a former  former neo-nazi and for the most part  they describe a situation of a childhood  that was missing something and sort of  situations of opportunity where someone  came to them with beliefs that were part  of a community they wanted community and  that that's how they sort of got sucked  in are those stories the exception or do  you find that they are predominant in  those circles no and I should mention  that since 2012 in addition to doing the  field work with active members we've  been interviewing formers like like  Scott and Frankie  we've now interviewed 104 former white  supremacists so we're able to least get  a sense of kind of different kinds of  patterns in terms of entry as well as  how folks end up leaving and you know I  think both of the factors that you point  to seem pretty consistent across the  board I mean again you know these you  know people for lots of different  reasons and in lots of different ways so  there's certain you know degree of  diversity in terms of how people get  involved in these groups as well as how  they leave but those are patterns that  we do see you know pretty commonly  emerge across the board and I should  also point out we also see folks talking  a lot about their childhood upbringing  in terms of family members who are not  members of these groups for the most  part I mean we do have a certain portion  of folks that we've interviewed who grew  up literally in families where you know  that from day one they were  indoctrinated from day one they were  socialized to be involved in these kind  of groups but that's fairly rare  fortunately but what folks do report  growing up around is kind of this more  everyday racism where they're hearing  you know the n-word they're hearing  other racial epithets and and and other  words negative words to describe  different racial ethnic religious groups  they're hearing ideas that kind of  communicate certain kind of homophobia  anti-semitism racism at home in a way  that's kind of somewhat innocuous you  might say and I think it desensitizes  them to the extent to where when they do  come in contact with these more extreme  groups that have more extreme beliefs  about these issues it's it's not as far  of a bridge to cross for them  because they've had this background and  of course unfortunately in our society a  lot of people grow up around that so  it's certainly not you know the only  factor but but I think it is an  important factor that we should keep in  mind in terms of that you know this very  pervasive everyday racism that continues  to persist and in our society when we  will talk in part two of the interview  about the current political climate but  before we get to that specifically sure  when it comes to people that get out of  these movements  when I speak to folks like Daryl Davis  for example the people he knows who get  out tend to be more on the side of they  reconsider their beliefs and they  realized that they were wrong or their  views are deplorable or discriminatory  whatever my sense is that that is  probably more of an exception and that  for a lot of people that get out it  relates more to it's causing a specific  problem with their significant other  it's causing an employment problem or  that type of thing where do you think  that the balance falls on that yeah Deb  a ston you know the day that we've  collected is definitely the ladder for  you know the majority of folks it's you  know people get involved for certain  reasons they think they're going to have  certain needs met you know in other  words when we have our needs met we tend  to have a higher level of satisfaction  with whatever it is we're involved in  and as people's needs are not met they  become dissatisfied with things that  they're involved in and in that sense  this is a lot like that that you know  people find a lot of hypocrisy in these  movements they find a lot of  backstabbing they find a lot of  interpersonal disputes and drama and  they find in many cases people are  saying one thing and doing another and  and so as these kind of kind of this  disenchantment starts to build people  start to you know say well what why am I  actually doing this is it really giving  me what I thought it would give me and  then that's where the distance from the  group starts to get generated it seems  and then later on in some cases folks do  start to really kind of evaluate their  beliefs and and by having distance from  the group that gives them a chance to  really rethink things now I should point  out in some cases people leave these  groups but they don't change their  beliefs you know I continue to have the  same beliefs they're just not  necessarily involved in a formal group  or something  let's pause our conversation there we've  been speaking with Peter simi who's  professor of sociology at Chapman  University co-author of the book with  Robert fat rel American swastika inside  the white power movements hidden spaces  of hate and we will pick up our  conversation with him right there

